comments.comment_body_raw:
@pohly yes, this is also a very good option (the provisioning is more invisible to the scheduler) and AFAIR it was called the proxy-option. It still can be an implementation detail of the _Upcoming_ -> _Actual_ transition without a need to predict/determine the final device name.

However, making the attachment (scale up) process eventually visible to the scheduler has some advantages, as in the future extensions the cost of attaching a device (time) could be taken into consideration during scheduling, similar to other costs like necessary preemption, node provisioning etc. It could also improve observability of the workload scheduling process.

It would also good to model scale up as a part of a new scheduling phase different than binding, as it is a process which can fail for legitimate reasons (fabric devices pool is shared between clusters), so having an intermediate step of reserving potential resource (with confirmation) earlier than during binding, would be highly desired. That's why we think about introducing some WaitOnResources gate (@wojtek-t).

